[2] Graph carriers requested by the prompt
  octahedron L(K4): {'vertices': 6, 'edges': 12, 'degree_hist': {4: 6}, 'triangles': 8, 'cycle_rank': 7}
    readout: six packet vertices are K4 edges; cycle rank 7 is the apex-7 curl module.
  Clebsch folded 5-cube: {'vertices': 16, 'edges': 40, 'degree_hist': {5: 16}, 'triangles': 0, 'cycle_rank': 25}
    common-neighbor hist adjacent={0: 40} nonadjacent={2: 80}
    closed-neighborhood design={'blocks': 16, 'block_size_hist': {6: 16}, 'point_rep_hist': {6: 16}, 'pair_rep_hist': {2: 120}}
  halved 5-cube: {'vertices': 16, 'edges': 80, 'degree_hist': {10: 16}, 'triangles': 160, 'cycle_rank': 65}
    identical to complement(Clebsch) under even representatives: True
  readout: Clebsch/half-cube is the cut/covariance side; octahedron is
           the support-six curl side.  Neither should be collapsed to a
           raw runner tournament.

[3] Paley-Zygmund / second-moment calibration
  Toy model: six independent empty-sector indicators with a=(6/7)^k.
  PZ lower-bounds P(N>0) by E[N]^2/E[N^2].  It is an existence tool,
  but the repo's live wide cap needs HYP-2823's degree-4 moment target.
  k   a          E[N]       exact P(N>0)  PZ bound   loss
   8   0.291357   1.748143      0.873362   0.711557 0.161805
   9   0.249735   1.498408      0.821643   0.666352 0.155291
  10   0.214058   1.284350      0.764310   0.620372 0.143938
  11   0.183479   1.100871      0.703650   0.574150 0.129499
  12   0.157267   0.943604      0.641789   0.528234 0.113555
  readout: second moment can guarantee nonzero witness mass, but it loses
           too much for tight LRC14 cap comparison unless upgraded to the
           labelled factorial-moment/Delsarte lane.

[4] Tournament Analysis on seven proof carriers
  vertices: proof carriers, not runners
  observable: (theorem scale, label retention, LRC relevance, scalarization risk)
  switch/gauge: lexicographically better role score wins
  tie Hamiltonian path: listed carrier order
    q_binding_scale        score=(4, 4, 4, 0) role=theorem coordinate
    p_plus_q_additive      score=(3, 3, 4, -1) role=n+2 / Stern-Brocot ledger
    p_times_q_product      score=(2, 4, 3, -2) role=multiplicative coimage side channel
    octahedron_LK4         score=(2, 4, 4, -1) role=support-six current / face-curl carrier
    Clebsch_halfcube       score=(1, 4, 3, -2) role=folded residual covariance carrier
    PZ_second_moment       score=(1, 2, 3, -3) role=existence lower-bound / moment gateway
    power_payloads         score=(0, 2, 2, -4) role=magnitude-leak stress tests
  fingerprint: score_hist={6: 1, 5: 1, 3: 1, 4: 1, 2: 1, 1: 1, 0: 1} c3=0
  Hamiltonian role order:
    q_binding_scale > p_plus_q_additive > octahedron_LK4 > p_times_q_product > Clebsch_halfcube > PZ_second_moment > power_payloads

[Proof readout]
  HYP-2931's denominator verdict survives: q is the theorem coordinate.
  The n+2 analogy is literal on unit-excess Farey children: q -> q+14.
  The n*2/product analogy should be a side-channel area/coimage ledger,
  not an ordering replacement for q.
  Octahedral and Clebsch/half-cube carriers explain where labelled packet
  covariance and curl data should go after the fraction address is kept.
  Paley-Zygmund is useful only as an existence gateway; for the cap route
  it must be upgraded to HYP-2823's degree-4 factorial moment inequality.
  Next theorem target: a reduced |M14|<=6 atom should carry the labelled
  address (e,q,p+q,p*q) plus Clebsch/octahedral packet data into either
  HYP-2930's non-tight Farey class or HYP-2908's forbidden H=7 state lift.
